<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saxony is a name that has moved across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It first meant the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in the north-west, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and later ruled as the Duchy of Saxony. After that duchy was broken up in 1180, the name and the electoral title passed to lands on the middle Elbe, held from 1423 by the Wettins, so that today's Free State of Saxony, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, lies in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxony.jpg" alt="Saxony" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxony.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
